\label{ch:proof-carrying}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:pcc-intro}

When untrusted code is loaded into a trusted system---a browser plugin, a kernel module, a mobile app---how can the system ensure that the code is safe? Traditional approaches rely on sandboxing, type checking, or bytecode verification. \emph{Proof-carrying code} (PCC) offers a more fundamental solution: the code producer attaches a formal proof that the code satisfies a safety policy, and the code consumer checks the proof before loading the code. If the proof is valid, the code is guaranteed to be safe.

This chapter explores the concept of proof-carrying code, the proof obligations for compiler optimizations, SMT-based validation, and the practical systems that implement these ideas.

\section{Proof-Carrying Code: Concept}\label{sec:pcc-concept}

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:pcc-problem}

When a code consumer (the operating system, a browser, a runtime) receives code from an untrusted producer, it must decide whether to execute the code. The options are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Trust the producer}: risky, the code may be malicious or buggy.
  \item \textbf{Sandbox the code}: restrict what the code can do, at the cost of functionality and performance.
  \item \textbf{Verify the code}: check that the code satisfies a safety policy. This is the PCC approach.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The PCC Protocol}\label{sec:pcc-protocol}

\begin{definition}[Proof-Carrying Code]\label{def:pcc}
\emph{Proof-carrying code} (PCC) is a mechanism in which:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The code \emph{producer} generates code and a proof that the code satisfies a safety policy $P$.
  \item The code \emph{consumer} receives the code and the proof.
  \item The consumer checks the proof using a \emph{proof validator} (a small, trusted component).
  \item If the proof is valid, the consumer loads and executes the code.
  \item If the proof is invalid, the consumer rejects the code.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

