\subsection{Key Properties}\label{sec:pcc-properties}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{No trust in the producer}: the producer does not need to be trusted; only the proof matters.
  \item \textbf{Small TCB}: the proof validator is small and can be carefully audited or even formally verified.
  \item \textbf{Flexible policies}: the safety policy can be customized for each consumer.
  \item \textbf{No runtime overhead}: once the proof is checked, the code runs at full speed (no sandboxing overhead).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Historical Context}\label{sec:pcc-history}

Proof-carrying code was introduced by Necula and Lee (1996) at Carnegie Mellon University. The original system used a type-theoretic approach: the proof was a derivation in a typed logical framework, and the proof checker was a type checker for that framework.

\section{Proof Obligations for Optimizations}\label{sec:proof-obligations}

\subsection{Optimization Correctness}\label{sec:opt-correctness}

When a compiler optimization transforms a program, it must preserve the program's semantics. This can be expressed as a proof obligation:

\begin{definition}[Optimization Proof Obligation]\label{def:opt-proof}
For an optimization that transforms program $P$ to program $P'$, the proof obligation is:
\[
  \forall \sigma.\; \sembrack{P}\sigma = \sembrack{P'}\sigma
\]
where $\sembrack{P}\sigma$ is the semantics of $P$ in state $\sigma$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Proof Obligations for Specific Optimizations}\label{sec:specific-obligations}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Proof obligations for common optimizations}
\label{tab:proof-obligations}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Optimization} & \textbf{Proof Obligation} \\
\hline
Constant folding & The constant value equals the expression's value. \\
Dead code elimination & The eliminated code has no observable effect. \\
Common subexpression elimination & The two expressions compute the same value. \\
Loop unrolling & The unrolled loop has the same semantics. \\
Instruction selection & The selected instruction computes the same value. \\
Register allocation & The allocated code respects the calling convention. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Generating Proof Obligations}\label{sec:generating-obligations}

The compiler can generate proof obligations as a byproduct of optimization:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The optimizer applies a transformation.
  \item The optimizer generates a proof obligation for the transformation.
  \item The obligation is discharged by an automated prover (SMT solver, theorem prover).
  \item If the obligation is discharged, the transformation is accepted.
  \item If not, the transformation is rolled back.
\end{enumerate}

\section{SMT-Based Validation}\label{sec:smt-validation}

\subsection{SMT Solvers}\label{sec:smt-solvers}

\begin{definition}[SMT Solver]\label{def:smt}
An \emph{SMT} (Satisfiability Modulo Theories) solver determines whether a logical formula is satisfiable with respect to a combination of background theories (e.g., linear arithmetic, bitvectors, arrays, uninterpreted functions).
\end{definition}

Common SMT solvers:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Z3} (Microsoft): the most widely used SMT solver.
  \item \textbf{CVC4/CVC5}: developed at Stanford and the University of Iowa.
  \item \textbf{Boogie}: a verification condition generator built on Z3.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Encoding Compiler Semantics in SMT}\label{sec:encoding}

To validate compiler optimizations, the semantics of the source and target programs are encoded as SMT formulas:

\begin{example}[Encoding Constant Folding]\label{ex:smt-constant-folding}
To verify that \texttt{x + 0} can be replaced by \texttt{x}:
\begin{verbatim}
  (declare-fun x () (_ BitVec 32))
  (assert (not (= (bvadd x (_ bv0 32)) x)))
  (check-sat)  ; Should return UNSAT (the negation is unsatisfiable)
\end{verbatim}
If the solver returns \texttt{UNSAT}, the optimization is correct for all values of \texttt{x}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Bounded Translation Validation}\label{sec:bounded-tv}

\begin{definition}[Bounded Translation Validation]\label{def:bounded-tv}
\emph{Bounded translation validation} validates a compiler optimization for all inputs up to a bounded size (e.g., all bitvector inputs of width $\leq 32$). This is incomplete (does not prove correctness for all input sizes) but can be fully automated.
\end{definition}

\section{Alive2 and Bounded Translation Validation}\label{sec:alive2}

\subsection{Overview}\label{sec:alive2-overview}

\begin{definition}[Alive2]\label{def:alive2}
\emph{Alive2} is a bounded translation validation tool for LLVM's optimization passes. It validates that each LLVM optimization correctly transforms the source IR to the target IR, for all inputs up to a bounded size.
\end{definition}

Alive2 was developed to address the problem of optimization bugs in LLVM. It works by:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Parsing the source and target LLVM IR (as optimization test cases).
  \item Encoding the semantics of both IR fragments as SMT formulas.
  \item Checking that the target refines the source (every behavior of the target is allowed by the source).
  \item Reporting any counterexamples (inputs where the optimization is incorrect).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Alive2's Impact}\label{sec:alive2-impact}

Alive2 has discovered dozens of previously unknown bugs in LLVM's optimization passes, including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Incorrect handling of \texttt{undef} and \texttt{poison} values.
  \item Incorrect handling of undefined behavior (e.g., signed overflow).
  \item Incorrect peephole optimizations.
  \item Incorrect instruction combining.
\end{itemize}

