\subsection{Poison and UB in LLVM}\label{sec:poison-ub}

One of Alive2's major contributions is clarifying the semantics of \texttt{poison} and undefined behavior in LLVM:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{undef}: a value that can be anything. Reading \texttt{undef} gives an unspecified value.
  \item \texttt{poison}: a value that makes any computation using it also \texttt{poison}. Unlike \texttt{undef}, \texttt{poison} is ``infectious.''
  \item \textbf{Undefined behavior}: certain operations (signed division by zero, out-of-bounds shift) cause undefined behavior, meaning the compiler can assume they never happen.
\end{itemize}

Alive2's formal model of these concepts has led to significant changes in LLVM's semantics.

\section{Certifying Compilers}\label{sec:certifying}

\subsection{From Validation to Certification}\label{sec:validation-to-certification}

Translation validation checks each compilation individually. A \emph{certifying compiler} goes further: it produces a proof with every compilation that can be checked independently.

\begin{definition}[Certifying Compiler]\label{def:certifying}
A \emph{certifying compiler} is one that produces, along with each compiled program, a proof that the compiled program correctly implements the source program's semantics.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Proof Formats}\label{sec:proof-formats}

The proof must be in a format that can be checked by a small, trusted proof checker:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{LF (Logical Framework)}: the original PCC approach, using a typed lambda calculus as the proof language.
  \item \textbf{SMT-LIB}: proofs generated by SMT solvers, checkable by an SMT proof checker.
  \item \textbf{Dedukti}: a universal proof format that can encode proofs from multiple systems.
  \item \textbf{ERCOF}: a proof format for compiler optimization verification.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Proof Checker}\label{sec:proof-checker}

The proof checker must be:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Small}: a small codebase is easier to audit and verify.
  \item \textbf{Trusted}: the entire security argument depends on the checker's correctness.
  \item \textbf{Fast}: proof checking should be much faster than proof generation.
\end{itemize}

Ideally, the proof checker itself is formally verified (e.g., using CompCert or a similar tool).

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Checking a Straight-Line Range Certificate}

A consumer can check a certificate without running the producer's search
algorithm. Consider a numerical DSL where a display channel may be driven
only by outputs between \(0\) and \(100\). The consumer derives that final
obligation from its own interface; it must not let a certificate declare
what the permissible range is. An example certificate gives an interval
for each individual value, rather than a claim about the final channel
alone.

\textbf{Contract.} The input is a well-formed acyclic list of
single-assignment definitions in dependency order and a proposed nonempty
closed integer interval \(C[i]\) for each definition. Instructions are
input reads, literals, addition, and subtraction. The trusted interface
supplies ranges for reads; arithmetic is exact. Acceptance guarantees the
designated output lies in \([0,100]\) for every admitted input. A
rejection does not establish that the output can leave that interval.

\begin{sudocode}[Check intervals against the instruction list]
\For{each definition $i$ in order}
  \To{Use the actual opcode, not one from the witness.}
  \If{$i$ reads input $x$}
    \Let{J}{\mathrm{InterfaceRange}[x]}
  \ElsIf{$i$ is literal $k$}
    \Let{J}{[k,k]}
  \Else
    \Let{J}{\Call{IntervalOp}{i,C}}
  \EndIf
  \To{A looser asserted interval is sound, a tighter one is not.}
  \If{$J\not\subseteq C[i]$}
    \State \Return reject
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return whether $C[\mathrm{out}]\subseteq[0,100]$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} For \(x\in[10,20]\), the code \(a=x+7,\ b=40-a\)
admits intervals \([17,27]\) and \([13,23]\). Subtraction uses
\([l_1-h_2,h_1-l_2]\); reversing the choice of endpoints would miss
possible outputs. The checker accepts this certificate. If the producer
claims \(b\in[14,23]\), it rejects at \(b\), independently of the fact
that the final claimed interval fits the channel.

The invariant is containment of every checked concrete value in its
asserted interval. Local inclusion and monotonicity of the transfer
functions establish it by induction. The checker uses a linear number of
interval operations. It may reject true claims when operands are
correlated: \(x-x\) is identically zero but interval subtraction here
produces \([-10,10]\). A more expressive certificate would need a
correspondingly stronger checker rule, not an exception to containment.

\section{Practical Considerations}\label{sec:practical}

\subsection{Proof Size}\label{sec:proof-size}

Proofs can be large:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A proof of correctness for a single optimization may be thousands of SMT assertions.
  \item A proof for an entire compilation may be megabytes.
  \item Proof compression and serialization are important practical concerns.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Proof Generation Time}\label{sec:proof-time}

Generating proofs adds overhead to compilation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item SMT solving can be slow for complex formulas.
  \item Proof generation may not scale to very large programs.
  \item Selective certification (certifying only critical code) may be practical.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Integration with Existing Compilers}\label{sec:integration}

Integrating PCC with existing compilers requires:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Formal semantics for the source and target languages.
  \item A proof generation infrastructure.
  \item A proof checking infrastructure.
  \item Modifications to the compiler to emit proofs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Applications}\label{sec:applications}

PCC and related techniques are applicable to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Verified compilation}: ensuring that optimized code is correct.
  \item \textbf{Secure loading}: ensuring that loaded code satisfies a security policy.
  \item \textbf{Bug detection}: finding optimization bugs in compilers.
  \item \textbf{Regularity enforcement}: ensuring that compiler output conforms to a specification.
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced SMT Validation}\label{sec:advanced-smt}

\subsection{Encoding Control Flow in SMT}\label{sec:smt-control-flow}

Validating optimizations that involve control flow requires encoding the program's control-flow structure:

\begin{example}[Encoding a Branch Optimization]\label{ex:smt-branch}
To verify that simplifying \texttt{if (x > 0 \&\& x < 10)} to \texttt{if (x > 0)} is correct when we know \texttt{x < 10}:
\begin{verbatim}
  (declare-fun x () (_ BitVec 32))
  ; Assume x < 10 (signed)
  (assert (bvslt x (_ bv10 32)))
  ; Check: does (x > 0 && x < 10) == (x > 0)?
  (assert (not (=
    (and (bvsgt x (_ bv0 32)) (bvslt x (_ bv10 32)))
    (bvsgt x (_ bv0 32)))))
  (check-sat)  ; Should return UNSAT
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Memory Reasoning in SMT}\label{sec:smt-memory}

Validating memory optimizations (e.g., load/store reordering, dead store elimination) requires encoding the memory model:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Encoding a store-load forwarding optimization:
  ; If we store value v to address a, then load from address a,
  ; the load returns v.
  
  (declare-fun mem () (Array (_ BitVec 32) (_ BitVec 32)))
  (declare-fun a () (_ BitVec 32))
  (declare-fun v () (_ BitVec 32))
  
  ; After store: mem' = store(mem, a, v)
  ; Load from a: result = select(mem', a)
  ; Check: result == v
  (assert (not (= (select (store mem a v) a) v)))
  (check-sat)  ; UNSAT - the optimization is correct
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Handling Quantifiers in SMT}\label{sec:smt-quantifiers}

Some verification conditions require quantifiers, which are harder for SMT solvers:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Loop invariants}: proving that a loop optimization preserves semantics may require universally quantified invariants.
  \item \textbf{Array properties}: proving properties about all elements of an array requires quantification over array indices.
  \item \textbf{Unbounded programs}: proving correctness for all input sizes requires quantification.
\end{itemize}

SMT solvers handle quantifiers using \emph{instantiation strategies} (e.g., E-matching, model-based quantifier instantiation), but completeness is not guaranteed.

\section{Alive2: Deep Dive}\label{sec:alive2-deep}

\subsection{Alive2's Semantic Model}\label{sec:alive2-semantics}

Alive2 defines a precise semantic model for LLVM IR that captures:

\begin{definition}[Alive2 Semantic Model]\label{def:alive2-semantics}
Alive2's semantic model defines the meaning of each LLVM instruction as a function from states to states, where a state consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A mapping from registers to values (including \texttt{poison} and \texttt{undef}).
  \item A memory state (a map from addresses to values, with alignment and provenance information).
  \item The current basic block and instruction pointer.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Refinement Checking}\label{sec:refinement-checking}

Alive2 checks \emph{refinement}: the target must produce a result that is allowed by the source's semantics.

\begin{definition}[Refinement]\label{def:refinement}
Target program $T$ \emph{refines} source program $S$ if for every input:
\begin{itemize}
  \item If $S$ produces a value $v$, then $T$ produces a value $v'$ that refines $v$.
  \item If $S$ produces \texttt{poison}, then $T$ can produce any value (including \texttt{poison}).
  \item If $S$ produces \texttt{undef}, then $T$ can produce any value of the correct type.
  \item If $S$ has undefined behavior, then $T$ can do anything.
  \item If $S$ does not return (diverges), then $T$ can either diverge or return any value.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Refinement for values:
  ; v' refines v iff:
  ;   v is poison => v' can be anything
  ;   v is a concrete value => v' must be the same value
  ;   v is undef (any value) => v' can be any value
  
  ; Refinement for memory:
  ; T's memory state refines S's if:
  ;   All reads from non-local memory return the same values
  ;   T does not access memory outside what S allows
  ;   T preserves alignment constraints
\end{verbatim}

