\subsection{Scaling Alive2}\label{sec:alive2-scaling}

Alive2 faces scalability challenges:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Large functions}: SMT encoding grows with function size. Mitigation: validate basic blocks individually.
  \item \textbf{Complex memory operations}: memory encoding is expensive. Mitigation: use abstract memory models.
  \item \textbf{Many optimization passes}: each pass must be validated separately. Mitigation: batch validation of related passes.
  \item \textbf{Timeout}: SMT solving may time out for complex queries. Mitigation: use bounded model checking with smaller bitwidths.
\end{itemize}

\section{Integrating PCC with Build Systems}\label{sec:pcc-build}

\subsection{Continuous Verification}\label{sec:continuous-verification}

Translation validation can be integrated into the build process:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{CI/CD integration}: every compilation in the CI pipeline is validated. If validation fails, the build is rejected.
  \item \textbf{Regression testing}: validation catches optimization bugs before they reach production.
  \item \textbf{Compiler development}: compiler developers run validation on their changes to catch introduced bugs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Selective Certification}\label{sec:selective-certification}

Full certification of every compilation is expensive. Selective certification certifies only critical code:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Selective Certification Policy}
\label{alg:selective-cert}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{ShouldCertify}{$\text{function}, \text{policy}$}
  \If{function.\text{is\_safety\_critical}}
    \State \Return \textbf{true}
  \EndIf
  \If{function.\text{is\_security\_sensitive}}
    \State \Return \textbf{true}
  \EndIf
  \If{function.\text{has\_optimization\_history}}
    \State \Return \textbf{true} \Comment{Previously had bugs}
  \EndIf
  \If{policy.\text{certify\_percentage} > \textsc{Random}()}
    \State \Return \textbf{true} \Comment{Random sampling}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return \textbf{false}
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:pcc-summary}

Proof-carrying code and translation validation provide powerful techniques for ensuring the correctness of compiled code. By attaching formal proofs to compiled programs, these techniques enable code consumers to verify safety properties without trusting the compiler or the code producer. Tools like Alive2 have demonstrated the practical value of these techniques, discovering real bugs in production compilers. As SMT solvers and proof infrastructure continue to improve, we can expect these techniques to become more widely used in both compiler development and secure code loading.

\begin{historical}
Proof-carrying code was introduced by Necula and Lee in 1996, using a type-theoretic framework for proof generation and checking. The idea was ahead of its time: SMT solvers were not yet powerful enough, and the overhead of proof generation was prohibitive. The modern revival began with the development of powerful SMT solvers (Z3, CVC4) and the recognition that compiler bugs are a serious problem. Alive (2013) and its successor Alive2 (2019) brought bounded translation validation to LLVM, discovering dozens of real bugs. The connection between PCC and compiler verification was further strengthened by work on certifying compilers and proof-carrying optimization. Today, the ideas behind PCC are finding practical application in compiler testing, optimization validation, and secure code loading, fulfilling the vision that Necula and Lee articulated more than a quarter century ago.
\end{historical}
